From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:44:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 247/259] CED benchmark: time both kernels on shared outputs
 over eight placed graph pairs

Triton against itself showed the old design's intervals too narrow: about one
row in five read faster or slower by chance. Two graphs of the same kernel
differ by up to a percent, the first replay of a graph is slow and the
reference always replayed first, and a large copy's time depends on where its
inputs land (one Triton copy took 9.7 to 24 us across placements).

Each case now checks exactness on separate outputs, then times both kernels
writing the same outputs, over eight instances: fresh inputs after a spacer of
seeded random size, both graphs replayed untimed first, and which graph comes
first alternating. The 95% interval comes from the eight instance differences.
A gated case that reads slower is measured again on fresh placements and fails
only if it reads slower twice. Triton against itself on the gated copies is
reported with every run as the interval's check.

The gate is serving shapes: the metadata kernels run on every step, decode
included; the row copies run only under a CED plan, which keeps at least 128
rows, so their smaller cases are reported without gating. The copies cover
every layout CEDState registers and a plan padded with -1 rows.
